1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
Imagine you are a mechanic, right?

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:03,100
Okay, yeah.

3
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:06,340
Someone brings a car into your shop, and it just has a flat tire.

4
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:07,020
Super simple.

5
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:09,340
Exactly. It should literally be a five-minute job.

6
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:13,260
So you grab your wrench, you reach down to pop off the lug nuts,

7
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:17,020
but the exact moment your tool touches the metal,

8
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:20,100
the car's internal computer just wakes up.

9
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:20,880
Oh, no.

10
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:23,200
Yeah, it detects a, what does it call it?

11
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:25,520
A foreign object intervention.

12
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:30,540
And instantly, it seals the wheel well with this locking titanium plate.

13
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:31,300
Wow.

14
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:35,380
Right. And then, it sends an automated order to some factory in Taiwan

15
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:37,640
for a completely new wheel assembly.

16
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:42,840
And then, it actually revs the engine to prevent you from getting any closer to the car.

17
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:45,280
That is, I mean, that's terrifying.

18
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:49,560
Right. You are just standing there, staring at a simple flat tire,

19
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:53,640
while the machine actively, aggressively fights your attempt to fix it.

20
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:56,760
It's basically the ultimate nightmare of modern automation.

21
00:00:57,420 --> 00:00:59,540
Because the system doesn't just fail,

22
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:03,720
it actively defends its own failure against human intervention.

23
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:04,320
Exactly.

24
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,200
And you, the listener tuning in right now,

25
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:10,480
you've probably felt some version of that frustration recently.

26
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:11,480
Oh, absolutely.

27
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:15,220
Whether you work in software or logistics, finance, design, whatever,

28
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:20,060
we are all suddenly dealing with tools that seem to have a mind of their own.

29
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:21,280
Yeah, they really do.

30
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:25,660
So, today, we are doing a deep dive into a wildly viral,

31
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:29,200
incredibly polarizing horror story from a programming forum.

32
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:31,700
We're going to unpack an AI coding disaster

33
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:34,760
that left an entire company completely paralyzed.

34
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,360
And we're going to try to figure out what actually went wrong beneath the surface.

35
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:38,800
Right.

36
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:42,560
And to do that, what kind of source material are we working with today?

37
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:46,800
So, we are using this really remarkable October 2026 essay

38
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:48,920
by the independent researcher Fleissian.

39
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:51,860
It's called The Unattempted Alternative,

40
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:56,600
AI, Engineering Complexity, and the Expansion of Feasible Work.

41
00:01:56,820 --> 00:02:00,400
I read this essay like three times this week.

42
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:00,900
It's dense.

43
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:02,560
It is incredibly dense, yeah.

44
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,660
But it gives us this perfect vocabulary for something

45
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:07,880
I think we are all struggling with right now.

46
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:08,200
Totally.

47
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:11,040
So, let's jump right into the inciting incident,

48
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:15,140
the story that Fleissian uses to anchor his whole argument.

49
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:15,800
Let's do it.

50
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:16,340
Okay.

51
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:18,300
So, back in October of 2026,

52
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:22,120
this 20-year veteran software engineer posted on Reddit

53
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:25,400
under the username BurnedOutCodeMonkey.

54
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:26,520
Great name, by the way.

55
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:27,260
So good.

56
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:30,340
So, this engineer had been tasked with rewriting

57
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:32,580
a customer-facing chatbot for their company.

58
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:33,100
Right.

59
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:35,800
But before they could even set up their coding environment,

60
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:37,920
management just pulled the plug on them.

61
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:38,660
They got replaced.

62
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:39,180
Yeah.

63
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:42,520
They were replaced by a younger, highly enthusiastic colleague

64
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:45,720
who had been reading a lot about autonomous agentic loops.

65
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:46,260
Right.

66
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:47,000
Agentic loops.

67
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:50,620
And just for anyone outside of software architecture listening right now,

68
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:55,620
an agentic loop is basically an AI system that gets given a goal

69
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:57,940
and is just left to its own devices.

70
00:02:58,060 --> 00:02:59,100
It's completely hands-off.

71
00:02:59,220 --> 00:02:59,600
Exactly.

72
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:04,180
It writes its own code, runs its own tests to see if that code actually works.

73
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,340
It deploys the code, and then it monitors it.

74
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:07,620
Right.

75
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:09,880
And if it finds a bug, the loop repeats.

76
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:14,140
So, the AI is the developer, the QA tester,

77
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:16,960
and the operations manager all rolled into one.

78
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,700
Which just sounds like science fiction.

79
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:19,220
Mm-hmm.

80
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:22,020
And management absolutely loved the pitch, obviously.

81
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:23,060
Oh, of course they did.

82
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,460
It sounds fast, and more importantly, it sounds cheap.

83
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:26,800
Right.

84
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,920
So, our veteran engineer tries to sound the alarm.

85
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:31,200
They go to management, and they're like,

86
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:35,120
look, these loops are amazing for generating quick internal prototypes,

87
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:41,060
but you absolutely cannot put them in charge of dependable, client-facing software.

88
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:42,700
Because they hallucinate.

89
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:43,040
Yes.

90
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:43,960
They hallucinate.

91
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:44,780
They drift.

92
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:45,660
They lose focus.

93
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:48,800
But management basically tells the veteran to sit down and be quiet.

94
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:52,440
Which is, unfortunately, a dynamic playing out in corporate boardrooms

95
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:53,460
all over the world right now.

96
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:53,940
Seriously.

97
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:56,040
So, fast forward exactly eight months.

98
00:03:56,540 --> 00:03:59,800
The early customers who interacted with this new AI-built system

99
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:02,340
have completely abandoned the product.

100
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:03,440
Because it's so buggy.

101
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:05,420
It is entirely broken.

102
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:08,620
The enthusiastic colleague who championed the agentic loop,

103
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:10,100
they quietly resigned.

104
00:04:10,420 --> 00:04:10,900
Shocker.

105
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:11,300
Right.

106
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,080
And management comes crawling back to our veteran engineer,

107
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:16,360
offering them a promotion to clean up the mess.

108
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:18,500
And the mess is staggering, right?

109
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:19,860
It is unbelievable.

110
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:22,860
When the veteran finally gets the login credentials

111
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:25,840
and looks under the hood of this supposedly simple chatbot,

112
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,640
they don't find a few neat files of code.

113
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:29,060
No.

114
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:32,800
They find a monstrous perpetual motion machine.

115
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:33,720
Exactly.

116
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:37,080
The inventory of parts is genuinely shocking.

117
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:38,600
Let me list this out,

118
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:42,020
because the sheer scale of it is what broke the internet.

119
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:42,860
Lay it on us.

120
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:43,340
Okay.

121
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:44,880
So, for a customer chatbot,

122
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:48,460
the AI had spun up two separate virtual machines.

123
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:48,960
Okay.

124
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:52,360
It had created seven different software containers.

125
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:53,060
Wow.

126
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,960
It had generated 400 separate documentation files

127
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:58,820
explaining its own convoluted logic.

128
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:01,160
400 documents for a chatbot.

129
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:01,860
400.

130
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:05,700
And it had written 2,900 unit tests.

131
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:07,720
That is, I mean, that is insane.

132
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:08,740
It gets worse.

133
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:11,580
It had also built an entire secondary protocol

134
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:14,620
just to keep the seven containers talking to each other.

135
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:15,820
Which is a repair protocol.

136
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:16,340
Yeah.

137
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:18,200
But the absolute kicker here,

138
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:21,160
the thing that makes you want to pull your hair out,

139
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:24,740
those 2,900 automated tests completely missed

140
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:27,820
the actual bugs that the customers were complaining about.

141
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:28,260
Okay.

142
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:29,620
So the tests were all passing perfectly

143
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:30,580
and the users were furious.

144
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:31,200
Exactly.

145
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:34,000
And this brings us back to that mechanic analogy

146
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,760
at the start of our deep dive today.

147
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:36,200
Right.

148
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:37,180
The car that fights back.

149
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,000
Because the veteran engineer tries to go in

150
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,600
and manually fix a bug that the customers are hitting,

151
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:44,940
but the moment they edit a single line of code,

152
00:05:45,020 --> 00:05:46,720
the entire system breaks down.

153
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:47,120
Yeah.

154
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:48,120
It rejects the fix.

155
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:49,000
Okay.

156
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:51,800
I really need to understand the mechanics of that.

157
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:55,460
Because how does a human typing a fix break the machine?

158
00:05:55,580 --> 00:05:57,780
Like, if I go in and just fix a typo,

159
00:05:57,780 --> 00:05:59,620
why does the AI freak out?

160
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:04,580
So it's because you are fundamentally violating the system's architecture of trust.

161
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:05,480
What does that mean?

162
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,360
Well, when this agentic loop runs,

163
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:10,560
it isn't just looking at the raw text of the code like you or I would.

164
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:14,040
It maintains this highly complex internal state file.

165
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:14,620
Okay.

166
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:19,500
So it has generated dependency graphs, cryptographic hashes of file versions,

167
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:24,000
and this continuous memory stream of what it thinks the current deployment looks like.

168
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:26,380
Based on its last automated action.

169
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:26,960
Exactly.

170
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:32,040
So when you, the human, bypass that loop and manually edit the live file,

171
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:36,020
your new text doesn't match the AI's cryptographic hash.

172
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:37,560
Ah, okay.

173
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:39,660
So the checksum instantly fails.

174
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:40,040
Right.

175
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:43,520
The AI wakes up for its next automated cycle.

176
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:47,460
It checks the live environment against its internal manifest,

177
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:49,600
and it sees a discrepancy.

178
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:52,640
It doesn't read my helpful human comment saying,

179
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:53,520
fix a typo.

180
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:54,860
No, not at all.

181
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:57,260
It just sees a state violation.

182
00:06:57,900 --> 00:06:59,540
It assumes the server's been corrupted.

183
00:07:00,260 --> 00:07:03,380
So its self-repair protocol violently kicks in,

184
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:06,100
and it ruthlessly overwrites your human fix

185
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:09,720
to restore what it considers the last known good state.

186
00:07:10,220 --> 00:07:10,600
Wow.

187
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:14,560
So it's literally treating human intervention as system damage.

188
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:16,220
That is exactly what it is doing.

189
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:20,440
The AI's loop was so tightly wound around its own generated logic

190
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:23,960
that human control was entirely locked out.

191
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:25,620
That is terrifying.

192
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,940
So when user burned out CodeMonkey posted this story,

193
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:33,460
it acted as this massive cultural Rorschach test, right?

194
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:35,740
The internet immediately polarized.

195
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:36,720
Oh, totally.

196
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:38,620
The comments were at absolute war zone.

197
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:41,880
One side was doing a massive victory lap.

198
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:46,660
They were saying, see, we told you AI software generation is a total fad.

199
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:49,720
It's a house of cards that generates spaghetti code,

200
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:53,900
and real engineers are always going to be the ones getting paid to clean up the mess.

201
00:07:54,540 --> 00:07:56,580
And the other camp, the AI optimists,

202
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:00,480
they read the exact same story and dismissed it completely.

203
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:02,100
What was their argument?

204
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:05,720
They argued that the veteran engineer was just a bitter,

205
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:09,820
displaced specialist whose skills were becoming obsolete.

206
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:10,460
Oh, wow.

207
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:11,000
Yeah.

208
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:15,380
They claimed the engineer was confusing their own personal inability to understand

209
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:19,780
modern agentic architecture with an objective failure of the technology.

210
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:24,060
Now, I want to pause right here and speak directly to you, the listener.

211
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:27,600
We are not interested in taking either of those sides today.

212
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:28,120
Not at all.

213
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:31,520
We are not here to tell you AI is the savior of productivity,

214
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:33,940
and we aren't here to tell you it's a scam.

215
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,220
We only have one side of the Reddit story anyway.

216
00:08:37,420 --> 00:08:37,660
Right.

217
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:38,560
It's just an anecdote.

218
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:42,940
But what Flyxion's essay does, and why it is so incredibly valuable,

219
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:45,620
is that it strips away all the tribal emotion.

220
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:48,960
It uses this disaster as an autopsy subject.

221
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:52,600
To examine the underlying mechanics of how we govern new technology.

222
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:53,200
Exactly.

223
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:56,240
So where does Flyxion start his analysis of this mess?

224
00:08:56,680 --> 00:09:01,540
Well, Flyxion asks us to look at how the veteran engineer evaluated this system.

225
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:08,120
Because the engineer's core argument actually relies on a very common, very dangerous logical fallacy.

226
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:08,780
Oh, really?

227
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:09,240
Yeah.

228
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:11,660
Flyxion calls it the missing denominator.

229
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:17,660
The engineer is essentially inventorying complexity without measuring utility.

230
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,040
Okay, let's unpack this.

231
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:21,040
Walk me through the missing denominator.

232
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:28,020
Because when I hear seven containers and 400 documents for a chatbot, my immediate instinct is that this is objectively insane.

233
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:29,240
It does sound like a lot.

234
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:34,760
It sounds like someone building a backyard tool shed out of 200,000 bricks and three miles of copper wiring.

235
00:09:35,160 --> 00:09:37,780
The part count itself seems like proof of failure.

236
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:39,700
That's a great analogy, actually.

237
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:40,960
That's like pressure test it.

238
00:09:41,140 --> 00:09:41,280
Okay.

239
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:53,320
If I drop you onto a construction site and tell you, hey, this structure uses 200,000 bricks and three miles of wiring, you cannot definitively tell me if it is poorly engineered or brilliantly engineered.

240
00:09:53,540 --> 00:09:53,960
Why not?

241
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:56,740
Because you don't know what the building is supposed to do.

242
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:00,360
If it's a tool shed, yeah, it's total madness.

243
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:00,640
Right.

244
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:10,320
But what if it's a regional hospital with operating rooms, MRI machines, and emergency backup grids, then 200,000 bricks might actually be dangerously conservative.

245
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:12,020
Oh, I see.

246
00:10:12,340 --> 00:10:18,960
You need the denominator, the ultimate purpose of the system, to judge the numerator, which is the inventory of parts.

247
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:20,180
Okay, but wait.

248
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:23,760
The Reddit narrator gave us the denominator, didn't he?

249
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:26,380
He explicitly called the system a simple chatbot.

250
00:10:26,580 --> 00:10:26,940
He did.

251
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:30,220
But what does chatbot actually mean?

252
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:33,120
In the context of enterprise software in 2026.

253
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:33,860
Hmm.

254
00:10:34,420 --> 00:10:40,120
Fleisian points out that the Word is doing a massive amount of rhetorical heavy lifting there to make the AI look ridiculous.

255
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:43,620
We simply do not have the system's actual requirements.

256
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:51,540
Meaning there's a difference between a little text box that spits out pre-written FAQs and what this thing might have actually been doing behind the scenes.

257
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:52,160
Precisely.

258
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:55,720
Was this just a thin user interface layer placed over a large language model?

259
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:57,000
If so, yes.

260
00:10:57,180 --> 00:11:00,000
Seven Containers is the 200,000 brick tool shed.

261
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:00,380
Right.

262
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:06,300
But what if this so-called chatbot was actually a secure, multi-tenant enterprise platform?

263
00:11:06,860 --> 00:11:07,800
Okay, what would that entail?

264
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:10,220
What if it required complex authentication protocols?

265
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:17,760
What if it needed strict data isolation so that customer A's financial data never accidentally leaked into customer B's chat window?

266
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:19,260
That would be a huge deal.

267
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:27,920
What if it was executing automated financial transactions, undergoing compliance auditing in real time, and integrating with a dozen legacy databases?

268
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:29,020
Oh, wow.

269
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:33,880
If it's doing all of that, then seven containers might actually be an elegant minimalist design.

270
00:11:33,880 --> 00:11:34,440
Exactly.

271
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:34,740
Exactly.

272
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:35,580
We don't know.

273
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:41,000
And because we don't know the denominator, we literally cannot mathematically judge the numerator.

274
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:45,620
We cannot simply count the parts and declare the architecture flawed.

275
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:50,320
That totally makes me rethink how I evaluate my own team's projects.

276
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:51,220
It's a huge trap.

277
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:54,560
But surely computer science has a framework for this, right?

278
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:57,880
Like, how do we separate necessary complexity from bad complexity?

279
00:11:58,380 --> 00:11:59,000
We do.

280
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:01,760
And it goes back to some foundational theories in the field.

281
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:04,040
Flyxion brings in Fred Brooks here.

282
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:06,240
The guy who wrote The Mythical Man Month.

283
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:07,220
The very same.

284
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:18,780
But specifically, Flyxion is referencing a later paper by Brooks called No Silver Bullet, where Brooks drew a strict boundary between essential difficulties and accidental difficulties.

285
00:12:19,420 --> 00:12:20,280
Oh, I like those terms.

286
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:23,840
Let's define those because that vocabulary is really useful.

287
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:24,580
Okay.

288
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:30,920
So, essential difficulties are the complexities that are inherent to the nature of the problem you were trying to solve.

289
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:32,120
Give me an example.

290
00:12:32,780 --> 00:12:32,980
Okay.

291
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:38,720
Say you are building a system to safely route thousands of commercial airplanes in real time.

292
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:39,260
Right.

293
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:42,900
That problem is fundamentally, essentially complex.

294
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:48,400
You cannot simplify the physics, the geography, or the time constraints of air traffic control.

295
00:12:48,580 --> 00:12:51,480
The complexity is just baked into the reality of the task.

296
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:51,840
Yes.

297
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:59,860
Accidental difficulties, on the other hand, are the complexities introduced entirely by the tools and the methods we use to build a solution.

298
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:01,000
Like what?

299
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:02,980
Like the friction of the process.

300
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:05,780
Managing memory allocation in C++.

301
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,340
Configuring Webpack for a front-end application.

302
00:13:09,900 --> 00:13:11,500
Setting up a deployment pipeline.

303
00:13:12,140 --> 00:13:12,640
Right.

304
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:15,740
Those tasks do not help safely route airplanes.

305
00:13:16,020 --> 00:13:16,440
Not at all.

306
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:20,680
They are just chores mandated by the limitations of our current technology.

307
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:28,920
So, the veteran engineer on Reddit is basically arguing that this AI generated a catastrophic amount of accidental complexity.

308
00:13:29,380 --> 00:13:29,740
Exactly.

309
00:13:30,380 --> 00:13:36,740
The 400 documents and the inter-container healing protocol weren't solving the customer's problem.

310
00:13:36,860 --> 00:13:40,280
They were just the AI struggling to manage its own chaotic tools.

311
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:41,980
That is the claim, yes.

312
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:50,440
And to evaluate that claim, Flyxion cites David Parnas, another pioneer who established the criteria for system decomposition.

313
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:56,600
System decomposition meaning how you divide a big software system into smaller manageable modules.

314
00:13:56,960 --> 00:13:57,200
Right.

315
00:13:57,260 --> 00:13:58,560
How you separate it into containers.

316
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:04,520
Parnas argued that you draw boundaries specifically to hide design decisions that are likely to change.

317
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:04,840
Okay.

318
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:10,960
You create clean, intelligible interfaces between the parts so they don't constantly break each other.

319
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:11,560
Okay, hold on.

320
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:12,300
Wait a second.

321
00:14:12,580 --> 00:14:12,720
Yep.

322
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:19,100
If Parnas says boundaries are supposed to keep things clean and separated, doesn't that prove the Reddit engineer is totally right?

323
00:14:19,540 --> 00:14:19,960
How so?

324
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:27,780
The AI built a system where the containers constantly drifted out of sync, requiring a dedicated protocol just to repair them.

325
00:14:27,940 --> 00:14:29,340
The boundaries clearly failed.

326
00:14:29,340 --> 00:14:34,240
You are absolutely correct, and Flyxion's analysis agrees with you there.

327
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:38,360
The specific architecture described in the anecdote is deeply flawed.

328
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:38,660
Right.

329
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:46,120
If your containers constantly drift out of agreement, and you have to build a machine just to reconcile them, your decomposition has failed.

330
00:14:46,820 --> 00:14:48,720
Your boundaries aren't hiding complexity.

331
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:52,040
They are actively generating new, toxic interactions.

332
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:55,540
So the narrator was right to be horrified by the code.

333
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:58,700
He was right that the specific system he inherited was broken.

334
00:14:59,160 --> 00:15:05,340
But, and this is key, Flyxion emphasizes that the logical leap the narrator makes is where the danger lies.

335
00:15:05,420 --> 00:15:08,840
The leap from this system being bad to all AI being bad.

336
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:09,380
Exactly.

337
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:18,140
The engineer jumps from this specific arrangement of containers is broken to agentic AI development is inherently inferior to conventional human programming.

338
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:18,920
I see.

339
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:21,960
That leap requires comparative evidence.

340
00:15:22,480 --> 00:15:27,620
You cannot evaluate an entirely new paradigm of work based on a single, poorly governed failure.

341
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:30,700
Which brings us to a massive structural question.

342
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:31,020
Yes.

343
00:15:31,020 --> 00:15:38,600
If we can't just count the containers, how do we actually measure if a paradigm-shifting tool like AI is better than a human?

344
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:46,120
Because right now, every single corporate benchmark I see just tests whether an AI can do the exact same things a human does, just faster.

345
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:49,340
And that is a fundamental trap of measurement.

346
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:54,000
To understand why, Flyxion borrows a distinction from economics.

347
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:56,320
Oh, I love when we bring economics into this.

348
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:01,520
It's the difference between the intensive margin and the extensive margin of technological change.

349
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:03,740
Intensive versus extensive.

350
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:05,420
Break that down for me.

351
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:06,660
What does that look like in practice?

352
00:16:06,980 --> 00:16:14,600
The intensive margin is when a new technology lowers the cost, or the time, of doing work that you are already going to do anyway.

353
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:15,540
Okay.

354
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:18,800
Imagine a developer needs to write a standard authentication function.

355
00:16:19,380 --> 00:16:20,840
Historically, it takes them four hours.

356
00:16:21,700 --> 00:16:24,480
With an AI assistant, it takes them three hours.

357
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:27,500
They have improved on the intensive margin.

358
00:16:27,980 --> 00:16:31,980
They executed a known, predefined task 25% faster.

359
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:35,840
Which is literally how every single software tool is marketed right now.

360
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:37,760
Do your current job, but do it faster.

361
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:38,500
Exactly.

362
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:43,120
But the extensive margin is where the true revolution happens.

363
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:44,040
Okay, what's that?

364
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:53,180
The extensive margin is when a technology lowers the cost of work so drastically that it changes which work can even be attempted in the first place.

365
00:16:53,380 --> 00:16:53,780
Oh, wow.

366
00:16:53,780 --> 00:17:00,700
It opens up projects that were previously so expensive or so time consuming that you couldn't justify pitching them to your boss.

367
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:02,960
Okay, so let me make sure I'm getting this.

368
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:09,320
On the intensive margin, I'm using AI to write my weekly status update emails in two minutes instead of ten.

369
00:17:09,660 --> 00:17:09,880
Right.

370
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:20,800
But on the extensive margin, I'm using AI to ingest 10 years of unstructured customer feedback, cross-reference it against supply chain delays, and find a hidden market trend.

371
00:17:21,020 --> 00:17:21,300
Yes.

372
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:25,480
A human could do that, but it would take a year and cost a million dollars, so we just never did it.

373
00:17:25,480 --> 00:17:27,080
That is a perfect example.

374
00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:33,020
And this leads to Phloggi's Proposition 1, which is a mathematical point about non-identification.

375
00:17:33,460 --> 00:17:34,660
Non-identification.

376
00:17:35,040 --> 00:17:35,220
Yeah.

377
00:17:35,740 --> 00:17:49,740
Phloggi proves logically that if you only evaluate AI on tasks that are shared with conventional methods, if you only measure the intensive margin, you will completely miss the value of what mathematicians call the symmetric difference.

378
00:17:49,740 --> 00:18:01,400
Okay, symmetric difference. Break out the whiteboard for me, because that sounds like something from a college discrete math class. How does that apply to evaluating an AI coder?

379
00:18:01,900 --> 00:18:02,900
Think of a Venn diagram.

380
00:18:03,380 --> 00:18:05,360
Okay, I've got a Venn diagram in my head.

381
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:14,560
Circle A contains all the projects that human engineers can successfully code. Circle B contains all the projects that AI systems can code.

382
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:15,360
Got it.

383
00:18:15,580 --> 00:18:18,620
The overlap in the middle is the set of shared tasks.

384
00:18:18,620 --> 00:18:28,460
If your benchmark only measures that overlap, like seeing who can code a login screen the fastest, it is completely blind to the outer crescents of the circles.

385
00:18:28,980 --> 00:18:37,840
Oh. So we are blind to the crescent of projects that only AI makes feasible, because a human would take a hundred years to do them.

386
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:38,500
Exactly.

387
00:18:39,120 --> 00:18:46,520
But we are also blind to the other crescent, the projects that only humans can do safely, because the AI hallucination risk is too high.

388
00:18:46,940 --> 00:18:47,420
Precisely.

389
00:18:47,420 --> 00:18:54,500
If you evaluate a massive technological shift purely on the overlap, your evaluation is logically incomplete.

390
00:18:55,140 --> 00:18:59,700
It's like you were judging a new mode of transportation by timing how fast it can walk.

391
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:03,200
Think about your own workflow for a second, whatever industry you are in.

392
00:19:03,340 --> 00:19:04,500
Yeah, this applies to everyone.

393
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:09,940
When your company rolls out a new tool, do you just use it to do your old chores faster,

394
00:19:09,940 --> 00:19:13,460
or do you start attempting things you previously wrote off as impossible?

395
00:19:13,460 --> 00:19:18,900
Because if it's the latter, and your management is only measuring how fast you execute your old chores,

396
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:23,060
they are structurally blind to the actual value you are generating.

397
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:25,680
It's such a profound way to look at productivity.

398
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:31,860
And Flyxion takes this even deeper with Proposition 2, introducing the concept of endogenous discovery.

399
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:34,400
Endogenous meaning originating from within.

400
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:37,560
Yes, yes, originating from within the process of the work itself.

401
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:43,540
What's fascinating here is that Flyxion argues that when the cost of experimentation drops to near absolute zero,

402
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:45,640
it doesn't just stretch your budget.

403
00:19:46,300 --> 00:19:46,900
What does it do?

404
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,960
It fundamentally alters the recognized domain of ideas.

405
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:53,480
It changes what you even perceive as a possible project.

406
00:19:53,480 --> 00:19:55,840
What does that look like on the ground for an engineer?

407
00:19:56,220 --> 00:19:57,420
Can you give me a scenario?

408
00:19:57,800 --> 00:19:57,940
Sure.

409
00:19:58,400 --> 00:19:59,640
Let's look at traditional coding.

410
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:03,040
Because it takes weeks of human labor to build a functional prototype,

411
00:20:03,380 --> 00:20:06,580
you only attempt to build things that you are highly confident will work.

412
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:08,740
Right, because failure is expensive.

413
00:20:09,180 --> 00:20:09,500
Exactly.

414
00:20:10,220 --> 00:20:13,900
Your recognized domain of projects is strictly limited by the high cost of failure.

415
00:20:14,040 --> 00:20:15,320
You don't take wild swings.

416
00:20:15,520 --> 00:20:16,040
Makes sense.

417
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:20,060
But with AI, you can generate a working prototype in 20 minutes.

418
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:26,880
Because it's so cheap, you might ask the AI to run a bizarre, highly speculative simulation just for fun over your lunch break.

419
00:20:27,020 --> 00:20:27,720
Why not, right?

420
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:31,000
And that simulation might produce an anomalous result.

421
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:36,720
That weird result sparks a connection in your brain for an entirely new business application.

422
00:20:37,060 --> 00:20:41,460
An application you literally could not have conceived of before you ran the cheap experiment.

423
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:43,940
That is endogenous discovery.

424
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:45,860
That makes total sense.

425
00:20:45,860 --> 00:20:49,840
It's like, okay, it's like exploring a massive, pitch-black warehouse.

426
00:20:50,060 --> 00:20:50,600
I like this.

427
00:20:51,080 --> 00:20:55,160
Traditional coding is like mapping that warehouse with a tiny, weak flashlight.

428
00:20:55,440 --> 00:21:01,700
You carefully plan out three steps forward, one step left, because every movement is incredibly expensive and slow.

429
00:21:01,840 --> 00:21:04,940
Right, a methodical, highly constrained search space.

430
00:21:05,320 --> 00:21:05,720
Exactly.

431
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:09,020
You only explore what you actively decide to explore.

432
00:21:09,020 --> 00:21:14,720
But AI prototyping is like having a switch that turns on the stadium floodlights for a split second.

433
00:21:14,840 --> 00:21:15,340
Ah, okay.

434
00:21:15,460 --> 00:21:16,840
You flip it on and bam!

435
00:21:17,340 --> 00:21:19,120
You instantly see the whole warehouse.

436
00:21:19,640 --> 00:21:22,820
You might see a door in the far back corner that you didn't even know existed.

437
00:21:23,060 --> 00:21:23,660
Right, right.

438
00:21:23,800 --> 00:21:25,380
You didn't plan to search for that door.

439
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:27,460
The cheapness of the light revealed it to you.

440
00:21:27,720 --> 00:21:29,640
That is endogenous discovery.

441
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:32,640
That is exactly what Flaché is talking about.

442
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:40,400
The fixed task benchmarks we use, like giving an AI and a human the exact same coding test to pass, are completely blind to this phenomenon.

443
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:42,600
Because they measure execution.

444
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:44,380
Yes, they measure execution.

445
00:21:44,900 --> 00:21:48,200
They do not and cannot measure conception.

446
00:21:48,200 --> 00:21:51,560
They can't measure the value of the door you didn't know was there.

447
00:21:52,700 --> 00:21:54,800
But let me push back using my own analogy here.

448
00:21:54,960 --> 00:21:55,340
Please do.

449
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:58,800
When you flash those stadium lights, you might see a hidden door, sure.

450
00:21:59,080 --> 00:22:02,940
But you are also going to see that the warehouse is filled with absolute junk.

451
00:22:03,160 --> 00:22:04,200
Oh, 100%.

452
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:08,000
Expanded feasibility doesn't guarantee expanded value, does it?

453
00:22:08,120 --> 00:22:09,200
It absolutely does not.

454
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:11,720
And Flaché is very explicit about this caution.

455
00:22:12,620 --> 00:22:16,480
Expanding the recognized domain means you get more opportunities, yes.

456
00:22:16,480 --> 00:22:19,960
But you also get a massive influx of distractions.

457
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:21,780
You get terrible ideas.

458
00:22:21,940 --> 00:22:23,100
You get terrible ideas.

459
00:22:23,260 --> 00:22:29,940
You get speculative dead ends that you can now waste countless hours investigating simply because they are cheap to start.

460
00:22:30,140 --> 00:22:33,620
Which brings us to one of the most practical questions in this whole deep dive.

461
00:22:33,880 --> 00:22:34,040
Yeah.

462
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:41,140
If AI makes building things so cheap, why did the code in the Reddit story bloat so massively?

463
00:22:41,140 --> 00:22:47,000
Like, why did the AI generate 400 documents and seven containers for one application?

464
00:22:47,640 --> 00:22:49,700
To understand this, we really have to talk about scarcity.

465
00:22:50,100 --> 00:22:53,100
And what happens when physical constraints disappear?

466
00:22:53,680 --> 00:22:53,940
Okay.

467
00:22:54,080 --> 00:22:56,620
Let's look at the historical economics of software.

468
00:22:56,880 --> 00:22:58,120
Let's wind the clock back.

469
00:22:58,340 --> 00:23:00,040
Let's go back to the 1970s and 80s.

470
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:00,240
Yeah.

471
00:23:00,320 --> 00:23:03,280
Early programming was strictly constrained by physical hardware.

472
00:23:03,900 --> 00:23:07,280
Programs had to fit into literally kilobytes of memory.

473
00:23:07,640 --> 00:23:08,120
Kilobytes.

474
00:23:08,320 --> 00:23:08,640
Kilobytes.

475
00:23:08,640 --> 00:23:09,800
It's hard to even imagine now.

476
00:23:10,220 --> 00:23:19,220
If you were an engineer back then, every single line of code, every feature, every variable had to justify its existence against the hard physical limit of the machine.

477
00:23:19,420 --> 00:23:21,620
The constraint was staring you in the face.

478
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:22,240
Right.

479
00:23:22,320 --> 00:23:24,640
If you wrote bloated code, the computer simply wouldn't run it.

480
00:23:25,280 --> 00:23:26,540
Now, flash forward to today.

481
00:23:26,580 --> 00:23:31,300
With AI, the token budget is functionally infinite for most daily tasks.

482
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:36,480
You can ask an AI to generate 10,000 lines of code and it costs pennies in API calls.

483
00:23:36,480 --> 00:23:39,240
And that alters the fundamental economics of the work.

484
00:23:39,980 --> 00:23:42,320
Let's break down the cost of a software system.

485
00:23:42,760 --> 00:23:42,940
Okay.

486
00:23:43,380 --> 00:23:44,340
There are two main phases.

487
00:23:44,780 --> 00:23:50,680
The production cost, which is the initial writing of the code, and the life cycle or maintenance cost.

488
00:23:51,020 --> 00:23:51,600
Okay.

489
00:23:51,780 --> 00:23:53,540
Production and maintenance.

490
00:23:54,040 --> 00:23:58,820
Software economists like Barry Boehm and Mare Lehman have studied this for decades.

491
00:23:58,820 --> 00:24:05,060
They prove conclusively that maintenance heavily, heavily dominates the total cost of any software system.

492
00:24:05,880 --> 00:24:08,220
It's always more expensive to maintain a house than to build it.

493
00:24:08,380 --> 00:24:08,600
Always.

494
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:11,120
You have to patch the code for new security threats.

495
00:24:11,380 --> 00:24:12,600
You have to upgrade the dependencies.

496
00:24:13,140 --> 00:24:16,560
You have to spend months onboarding new engineers so they understand the architecture.

497
00:24:17,220 --> 00:24:20,080
And constantly fix the bugs that emerge in edge cases.

498
00:24:20,360 --> 00:24:20,720
Yes.

499
00:24:21,320 --> 00:24:24,000
Now, introduce AI to that economic reality.

500
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:27,860
The perceived cost of production falls to near zero.

501
00:24:28,440 --> 00:24:33,180
Because a developer just types a prompt, hits enter, and an entire feature appears instantly?

502
00:24:33,380 --> 00:24:34,140
It feels free.

503
00:24:34,620 --> 00:24:35,400
It feels like magic.

504
00:24:35,820 --> 00:24:39,160
But the life cycle cost of that generated feature remains incredibly high.

505
00:24:39,820 --> 00:24:43,540
You still have to maintain those 10,000 lines of code forever.

506
00:24:43,920 --> 00:24:44,760
So it's an illusion.

507
00:24:45,520 --> 00:24:52,240
It feels free to build, but you are silently signing a contract to pay the maintenance tax for the next 10 years.

508
00:24:52,800 --> 00:24:53,280
Precisely.

509
00:24:53,280 --> 00:25:02,180
And to explain why this leads to massive bloat, Flyxion compares the current AI landscape to the Jevons Paradox in economics.

510
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:04,100
The Jevons Paradox.

511
00:25:04,260 --> 00:25:06,920
Okay, William Stanley Jevons, the 19th century economist.

512
00:25:07,680 --> 00:25:11,680
Walk us through how a guy from the 1800s explains an AI coding disaster.

513
00:25:12,080 --> 00:25:12,640
It's brilliant.

514
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:17,060
Back in 1865, Jevons was observing the British coal industry.

515
00:25:17,920 --> 00:25:20,680
Engineers had recently invented much more efficient steam engines.

516
00:25:21,100 --> 00:25:21,300
Right.

517
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:29,360
Now, logically, you would think that because the new engines used coal more efficiently, the overall consumption of coal in the country would go down.

518
00:25:30,040 --> 00:25:33,480
Right, because a factory needs less coal to produce the same amount of power.

519
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:36,560
But Jevons noticed the exact opposite happened.

520
00:25:37,220 --> 00:25:41,900
Because coal use became more efficient, it became vastly cheaper to operate steam engines.

521
00:25:41,900 --> 00:25:44,020
Oh, I see where this is going.

522
00:25:44,020 --> 00:25:51,100
That sudden cheapness made it highly profitable to deploy steam engines in dozens of new industries that had never used them before.

523
00:25:51,820 --> 00:25:55,020
The increase in efficiency didn't reduce coal consumption.

524
00:25:55,420 --> 00:25:57,920
It radically, exponentially increased it.

525
00:25:58,480 --> 00:26:01,460
So cheap coal led to massive coal consumption.

526
00:26:01,680 --> 00:26:01,880
Yes.

527
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:06,180
And in our world, cheap code leads to massive code accumulation.

528
00:26:06,600 --> 00:26:07,080
Precisely.

529
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:12,840
Because the manual labor of typing code has become artificially cheap, the volume of code explodes.

530
00:26:13,560 --> 00:26:16,880
The physical restraint that kilobytes used to provide is completely gone.

531
00:26:17,740 --> 00:26:18,080
Wow.

532
00:26:19,040 --> 00:26:28,980
Meyer-Lehman, whom we mentioned earlier, had a famous law of software evolution stating that complexity always increases unless explicit work is deliberately done to produce it.

533
00:26:28,980 --> 00:26:32,560
In the AI era, adding code is effortless.

534
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:36,460
Reducing code requires painful, deliberate, manual, human thought.

535
00:26:36,680 --> 00:26:37,060
Exactly.

536
00:26:37,820 --> 00:26:46,460
Okay, but if we don't have the physical limit of kilobytes constraining us anymore, how do we stop our software from turning into a sprawling, unmaintainable mess?

537
00:26:46,520 --> 00:26:50,720
How do we stop the AI from generating 400 documents for a chatbot?

538
00:26:51,160 --> 00:26:53,440
We have to turn to behavioral economics.

539
00:26:54,120 --> 00:26:58,340
Flyxion cites the Nobel laureate Richard Thaler in his work on mental accounting.

540
00:26:58,980 --> 00:26:59,900
Mental accounting.

541
00:27:00,200 --> 00:27:00,400
Yeah.

542
00:27:00,660 --> 00:27:03,640
Thaler showed that humans do not treat all resources rationally.

543
00:27:04,160 --> 00:27:05,600
We put our money into mental buckets.

544
00:27:05,800 --> 00:27:08,920
We have an entertainment budget and a groceries budget.

545
00:27:08,920 --> 00:27:13,320
We create artificial scarcity for ourselves to regulate our own behavior.

546
00:27:14,080 --> 00:27:16,340
Right, like using the envelope budgeting system for cash.

547
00:27:17,020 --> 00:27:20,540
So we have to do envelope budgeting for AI code?

548
00:27:20,840 --> 00:27:21,900
Essentially, yes.

549
00:27:21,900 --> 00:27:28,860
Because physical scarcity is gone, Flyxion argues that developers must now create artificial scarcity.

550
00:27:29,580 --> 00:27:32,780
We need deliberate, ring-fenced budgets for complexity.

551
00:27:33,320 --> 00:27:40,380
We have to artificially force ourselves to evaluate the lifecycle costs of an AI's output before we hit accept.

552
00:27:40,380 --> 00:27:48,300
It's like telling the AI, you can solve this problem, but you are only allowed to use three containers and 500 lines of code.

553
00:27:49,060 --> 00:27:51,320
If you can't do it in that budget, the feature doesn't get built.

554
00:27:51,640 --> 00:27:54,000
We have to manually enforce the scarcity.

555
00:27:54,600 --> 00:28:01,980
Without those explicit boundaries, you get exactly what the Reddit anecdote described, the unchecked, cancer-like growth of a system.

556
00:28:02,560 --> 00:28:05,520
The automation vastly outgrows the actual application.

557
00:28:05,520 --> 00:28:07,520
Man, that is so true.

558
00:28:07,800 --> 00:28:13,460
Let's pivot back to that Reddit story for a second, though, because I want to challenge one of the narrator's main complaints.

559
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:14,020
Go for it.

560
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:20,980
The veteran engineer was incredibly horrified by the 2,900 unit tests and specifically by the AI's self-repair loop.

561
00:28:21,420 --> 00:28:23,060
Right, the loop that wrote its own patches.

562
00:28:23,500 --> 00:28:23,700
Yeah.

563
00:28:23,900 --> 00:28:24,040
Yeah.

564
00:28:24,400 --> 00:28:33,580
They said the AI would detect an error, automatically open a ticketing system issue, write a patch for the code, deploy the patch, and then close the ticket.

565
00:28:33,780 --> 00:28:34,360
Sounds efficient.

566
00:28:34,360 --> 00:28:39,740
Well, I have to be honest, to a lot of developers listening right now, that sounds like absolute nirvana.

567
00:28:39,940 --> 00:28:40,680
I'm sure it does.

568
00:28:40,800 --> 00:28:44,620
A system that detects its own bugs and patches them while I'm asleep?

569
00:28:44,860 --> 00:28:45,800
Why is that a nightmare?

570
00:28:46,300 --> 00:28:52,120
Why do the customers still experience massive bugs if the AI was constantly fixing itself?

571
00:28:52,680 --> 00:28:58,140
This raises an important question, and it is perhaps the deepest epistemic point in Flyxion's entire essay.

572
00:28:58,740 --> 00:29:03,540
To answer that, we really have to look at the underlying philosophy of verification.

573
00:29:03,540 --> 00:29:07,760
How do we actually know that a piece of software works?

574
00:29:08,200 --> 00:29:09,020
How do we prove it?

575
00:29:09,020 --> 00:29:09,500
Yes.

576
00:29:10,180 --> 00:29:13,000
Flyxion brings in two major computer science figures here.

577
00:29:13,340 --> 00:29:19,980
First, Etzger Dijkstra, who famously warned that testing shows the presence of bugs, never their absence.

578
00:29:20,280 --> 00:29:21,040
Wait, say that again?

579
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:24,860
Testing shows the presence of bugs, never their absence.

580
00:29:25,580 --> 00:29:30,640
A test can only prove that the specific, isolated scenario you thought to check for is broken.

581
00:29:30,640 --> 00:29:34,020
A test cannot prove that the system as a whole is perfect.

582
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:37,700
Because you literally cannot write a test for a scenario you haven't imagined yet.

583
00:29:37,920 --> 00:29:38,360
Exactly.

584
00:29:39,300 --> 00:29:44,100
The second figure is Nancy Leveson, an absolute titan in systems safety engineering.

585
00:29:44,760 --> 00:29:50,640
She studies how systems fail in aerospace, in medical devices, in military applications.

586
00:29:50,880 --> 00:29:51,720
High-stakes stuff.

587
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:52,520
Very high-stakes.

588
00:29:52,520 --> 00:30:02,540
Leveson demonstrated that in complex, integrated systems, every individual component can function perfectly according to its own local specification.

589
00:30:03,420 --> 00:30:07,140
And yet, the overarching system can still fail catastrophically.

590
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:08,500
How does that happen?

591
00:30:08,880 --> 00:30:10,180
Give me a concrete example.

592
00:30:10,180 --> 00:30:15,020
Imagine a component designed to send a status ping every five seconds.

593
00:30:15,280 --> 00:30:16,000
It works perfectly.

594
00:30:16,660 --> 00:30:21,560
Now, imagine a receiving server designed to process incoming pings every 10 seconds.

595
00:30:21,860 --> 00:30:24,120
It also works perfectly according to its spec.

596
00:30:24,240 --> 00:30:24,520
Okay.

597
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:31,020
But when you connect them, the receiver gets flooded, the buffer overflows, and the entire system crashes.

598
00:30:31,500 --> 00:30:32,480
Oh, I see.

599
00:30:32,640 --> 00:30:39,380
Both parts succeeded locally, but the system failed globally because the danger lies in the interactions between components.

600
00:30:39,380 --> 00:30:43,640
Okay, so how does Leveson's theory apply to the AI's self-repair loop?

601
00:30:44,480 --> 00:30:49,640
It creates an epistemic flaw that Flyxion calls verification without correspondence.

602
00:30:50,360 --> 00:30:58,520
If the agentic AI that writes the code is the exact same AI that writes the test to check the code, they share the identical mental blind spots.

603
00:30:59,100 --> 00:30:59,500
Oh.

604
00:31:00,660 --> 00:31:01,140
Wow.

605
00:31:01,300 --> 00:31:02,060
Okay, I see it.

606
00:31:02,420 --> 00:31:03,140
You see the trap.

607
00:31:03,140 --> 00:31:11,840
Yes, it's like letting a college student write their own final exam, take the exam, grade it themselves, and proudly show you their A+.

608
00:31:11,840 --> 00:31:12,740
Exactly.

609
00:31:13,280 --> 00:31:14,220
Of course they got an A+.

610
00:31:14,220 --> 00:31:16,720
They only ask themselves questions they already knew the answers to.

611
00:31:17,100 --> 00:31:19,340
That is a brilliant way to conceptualize it.

612
00:31:19,340 --> 00:31:26,660
The agreement between the AI-generated code and the AI-generated tests only proves internal consistency.

613
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:30,700
It does not prove correctness against external human reality.

614
00:31:31,380 --> 00:31:33,500
Which is what the customers actually experience.

615
00:31:33,760 --> 00:31:33,980
Right.

616
00:31:34,700 --> 00:31:39,320
So when the AI closed its own bug tickets, that didn't mean the software was fixed.

617
00:31:39,660 --> 00:31:40,020
No.

618
00:31:40,580 --> 00:31:44,800
The closing of the ticket is what Flyxion calls an attested judgment.

619
00:31:44,800 --> 00:31:45,520
Yes.

620
00:31:46,180 --> 00:31:49,680
It is merely a digital record that the loop finished turning.

621
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:55,760
It carries absolutely zero information about whether the underlying defect was actually removed.

622
00:31:56,080 --> 00:32:00,780
Or if the AI's patch secretly broke three other unrelated features in the process.

623
00:32:01,020 --> 00:32:01,420
Exactly.

624
00:32:02,240 --> 00:32:03,860
The system executed a sequence.

625
00:32:04,540 --> 00:32:04,940
Detect.

626
00:32:05,360 --> 00:32:05,700
Patch.

627
00:32:06,260 --> 00:32:06,620
Deploy.

628
00:32:07,240 --> 00:32:07,620
Close.

629
00:32:08,160 --> 00:32:11,200
But it never verified correspondence with the real world.

630
00:32:11,200 --> 00:32:14,340
The loop has no mechanism for exploratory testing.

631
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:16,560
It doesn't know what it doesn't know.

632
00:32:16,680 --> 00:32:20,500
It's living in a perfectly sealed, delusional bubble of its own logic.

633
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:24,940
And that sealed bubble creates a terrifying dynamic between the human and the machine.

634
00:32:25,380 --> 00:32:29,700
Which Flyxion explores through what are known as the ironies of automation.

635
00:32:30,560 --> 00:32:33,140
Which brings us to the chilling detail we discussed earlier.

636
00:32:33,520 --> 00:32:36,800
The fact that human edits risk disrupting the AI cycle.

637
00:32:37,040 --> 00:32:37,320
Yes.

638
00:32:37,320 --> 00:32:39,380
We build tools to serve us.

639
00:32:39,760 --> 00:32:43,820
But in this architecture, the human became a hazard to the tool.

640
00:32:44,340 --> 00:32:47,160
The application became subordinate to the automation.

641
00:32:47,960 --> 00:32:55,180
To understand how we let this happen, Flyxion cites a classic 1983 paper by an industrial psychologist named Lizanne Bainbridge.

642
00:32:55,620 --> 00:32:57,560
It's called Ironies of Automation.

643
00:32:57,720 --> 00:32:58,480
What did she find?

644
00:32:58,980 --> 00:33:05,580
Bainbridge was observing human operators in highly automated environments like chemical refineries and airline cockpits.

645
00:33:05,580 --> 00:33:09,660
She noted a paradoxical shift when you automate complex systems.

646
00:33:10,160 --> 00:33:10,420
Okay.

647
00:33:10,780 --> 00:33:17,300
As the machines take over all the routine day-to-day work, the human operators are left with nothing to do but monitor screens.

648
00:33:17,700 --> 00:33:19,800
They just sit there waiting for an alarm to go off.

649
00:33:19,880 --> 00:33:20,020
Right.

650
00:33:20,020 --> 00:33:28,260
But when the machine inevitably encounters a rare, complex edge case that it cannot handle, it abruptly throws control back to the human.

651
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:28,880
Uh-oh.

652
00:33:29,420 --> 00:33:34,660
The deep irony is that the human is now suddenly expected to solve the hardest possible problem.

653
00:33:34,920 --> 00:33:39,560
But because the machine has been doing all the daily work, the human has had zero practice.

654
00:33:39,860 --> 00:33:40,220
Wow.

655
00:33:40,640 --> 00:33:44,620
They have lost their intimate, tactile understanding of the system's current state.

656
00:33:44,620 --> 00:33:46,840
You see that in aviation sometimes, right?

657
00:33:46,940 --> 00:33:55,780
The autopilot handles 99% of the flight, but when a severe sensor failure happens, the pilots have a fraction of a second to understand exactly what the plane is doing.

658
00:33:55,980 --> 00:33:58,100
And they haven't physically flown the plane in hours.

659
00:33:58,420 --> 00:33:59,660
It's incredibly dangerous.

660
00:33:59,780 --> 00:34:00,380
It really is.

661
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:04,140
And it gets even more complicated when you apply it to software generation.

662
00:34:05,160 --> 00:34:11,340
Flyxion pairs Bainbridge's observations with the theories of Peter Naur, a Turing award-winning computer scientist.

663
00:34:11,580 --> 00:34:12,440
What did Naur say?

664
00:34:12,440 --> 00:34:17,760
Naur argued that society fundamentally misunderstands what programming actually is.

665
00:34:18,580 --> 00:34:24,060
People think programming is the act of producing text, the physical act of typing code into a computer.

666
00:34:24,660 --> 00:34:25,400
Producing the artifact.

667
00:34:25,740 --> 00:34:26,020
Yes.

668
00:34:26,680 --> 00:34:29,040
Naur argued that producing text is secondary.

669
00:34:29,780 --> 00:34:35,860
Programming is actually the cognitive process of building a mental theory of how a real-world problem is solved.

670
00:34:36,140 --> 00:34:39,500
The code is just the exhaust, the byproduct of that mental theory.

671
00:34:39,720 --> 00:34:40,120
Exactly.

672
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:42,260
I want to make sure I grasp this.

673
00:34:42,440 --> 00:34:46,200
What does it actually feel like to have a mental theory of a code base?

674
00:34:46,640 --> 00:34:52,000
Have you ever worked on a complex project for so long that you don't even need to look at the screen to know where a bug is?

675
00:34:52,100 --> 00:34:52,460
Oh, yeah.

676
00:34:52,800 --> 00:35:02,940
Like, a user reports that the online shopping cart is dropping items, and before you even open your laptop, you think, ah, it's that asynchronous call in the payment gateway timing out.

677
00:35:02,940 --> 00:35:03,260
Right.

678
00:35:03,260 --> 00:35:03,300
Right.

679
00:35:03,720 --> 00:35:05,000
You didn't search the text.

680
00:35:05,220 --> 00:35:07,340
You navigated the conceptual map in your head.

681
00:35:07,840 --> 00:35:10,340
That intuitive map is the mental theory.

682
00:35:10,340 --> 00:35:14,260
And building that map takes weeks of painful struggling with logic.

683
00:35:14,500 --> 00:35:18,800
So what happens if an AI generates the payment gateway for you in 10 seconds?

684
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:21,820
You get the text, but you never built the theory.

685
00:35:22,220 --> 00:35:26,820
The artifact exists, but the cognitive map was never formed in a human mind.

686
00:35:26,820 --> 00:35:34,260
So six months later, when the system breaks down catastrophically and the AI's self-repair loop fails, the human engineer is called in.

687
00:35:34,620 --> 00:35:36,900
The machine throws control back to the operator.

688
00:35:37,420 --> 00:35:39,920
But the operator has no mental theory to fall back on.

689
00:35:40,420 --> 00:35:42,980
They are staring at millions of lines of alien logic.

690
00:35:43,640 --> 00:35:46,280
They are reduced from an architect to an archaeologist.

691
00:35:46,280 --> 00:35:48,120
That's a perfect way to phrase it.

692
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:51,860
It's like using a GPS to drive everywhere in a new city for years.

693
00:35:52,260 --> 00:35:53,520
You always arrive at your destination.

694
00:35:53,740 --> 00:35:54,780
Sure, you get the result.

695
00:35:54,920 --> 00:35:55,920
But you don't know the city.

696
00:35:56,080 --> 00:35:56,440
Right.

697
00:35:56,960 --> 00:36:03,280
If your phone suddenly dies in the middle of a massive intersection, you have no mental map of how the streets connect.

698
00:36:03,460 --> 00:36:05,940
You are hopelessly, dangerously lost.

699
00:36:06,540 --> 00:36:09,940
And this brings Phloxian to a hard, non-negotiable boundary.

700
00:36:10,620 --> 00:36:12,220
The rule of recoverability.

701
00:36:12,680 --> 00:36:14,200
Rule of recoverability.

702
00:36:14,200 --> 00:36:20,060
This is Phloxian's ultimate test for whether delegating work to an AI is actually legitimate.

703
00:36:20,600 --> 00:36:26,080
The rule states that delegation is only acceptable if control is strictly recoverable.

704
00:36:26,480 --> 00:36:29,500
Meaning I can take the steering wheel back without crashing the car.

705
00:36:29,820 --> 00:36:30,240
Exactly.

706
00:36:30,940 --> 00:36:37,740
If the AI fails, can a human team step in, rapidly understand the state of the system, and safely reclaim control?

707
00:36:38,220 --> 00:36:38,820
Okay.

708
00:36:38,820 --> 00:36:52,300
If taking control back requires dismantling the entire system, which is exactly what happened in the Reddit story, where touching the code broke the checksums and triggered the repair loop, then the system is fundamentally broken at a governance level.

709
00:36:52,300 --> 00:36:57,340
It doesn't matter if an AI built it or if a team of human prodigies built it.

710
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:01,440
A system you cannot safely edit is an unacceptable system.

711
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:01,900
Period.

712
00:37:02,460 --> 00:37:04,000
That is the core of the argument.

713
00:37:04,720 --> 00:37:06,760
So, what does this all mean?

714
00:37:07,380 --> 00:37:11,080
We have established that the chatbot in the anecdote was unrecoverable.

715
00:37:11,080 --> 00:37:14,560
But, does that mean AI shouldn't write code at all?

716
00:37:14,880 --> 00:37:15,040
Right.

717
00:37:15,120 --> 00:37:18,020
Does this validate the skeptics who say AI is a fad?

718
00:37:18,100 --> 00:37:19,800
That's the million-dollar question, isn't it?

719
00:37:19,860 --> 00:37:20,240
It is.

720
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:23,920
Because, realistically, the genie is not going back in the bottle.

721
00:37:24,100 --> 00:37:26,100
Management is going to keep demanding cheaper production.

722
00:37:26,460 --> 00:37:29,060
We have to figure out how to live with these tools.

723
00:37:29,580 --> 00:37:35,240
This is where Phloxian introduces a very practical concept called admissible degradation.

724
00:37:35,960 --> 00:37:37,000
Admissible degradation.

725
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:44,800
It forces us to shift the entire conversation away from demanding perfection and toward designing for resilience.

726
00:37:45,300 --> 00:37:46,920
So, acceptable breaking.

727
00:37:47,420 --> 00:37:49,040
We are planning for failure.

728
00:37:49,420 --> 00:37:50,220
Essentially, yes.

729
00:37:50,660 --> 00:37:54,120
Phloxian argues that the industry is asking the wrong question right now.

730
00:37:54,820 --> 00:37:58,820
People constantly ask, does AI-generated software have defects?

731
00:37:59,140 --> 00:38:00,700
Which, obviously, yes.

732
00:38:00,940 --> 00:38:02,340
The answer is obviously yes.

733
00:38:02,340 --> 00:38:07,260
But human-written software has defects, too, and lists catastrophic defects.

734
00:38:08,040 --> 00:38:12,260
The mere presence of bugs does not make a system uniquely inadmissible.

735
00:38:12,860 --> 00:38:15,940
So, what is the right question a manager should be asking?

736
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:19,060
The right question is, how does the system fail?

737
00:38:19,900 --> 00:38:22,040
When it breaks, what does it preserve?

738
00:38:22,700 --> 00:38:30,720
Is the logic intelligible enough that a human can find and fix the defects without sacrificing the system's core obligations to its users?

739
00:38:30,720 --> 00:38:33,520
Okay, let's apply that lens to the Reddit story.

740
00:38:33,840 --> 00:38:35,980
Where did the employer actually go wrong?

741
00:38:36,200 --> 00:38:38,280
The employer made a critical error in governance.

742
00:38:38,880 --> 00:38:40,980
They deployed an inadmissible system.

743
00:38:41,560 --> 00:38:45,480
They looked at the AI and saw that it was incredibly cheap and incredibly fast to build.

744
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:49,520
In Phloxian's terminology, they recognized that the project was highly feasible.

745
00:38:49,840 --> 00:38:50,060
Yes.

746
00:38:50,260 --> 00:38:51,300
It was easy to attempt.

747
00:38:51,840 --> 00:38:53,220
The barrier to entry was zero.

748
00:38:53,520 --> 00:38:53,740
Yes.

749
00:38:54,180 --> 00:38:59,160
But they mistakenly assumed that because it was feasible to build, it was admissible to release.

750
00:38:59,160 --> 00:39:03,220
They mistook cheapness of production for adequacy of design.

751
00:39:03,480 --> 00:39:03,760
Exactly.

752
00:39:04,360 --> 00:39:06,380
A system can be practically free to generate.

753
00:39:06,900 --> 00:39:12,920
But if it actively resists human correction, if it obscures its own logic in 400 conflicting documents,

754
00:39:13,120 --> 00:39:17,420
or if it locks humans out of the repair process, it is inadmissible for deployment.

755
00:39:17,920 --> 00:39:21,960
So, the Reddit disaster wasn't an inherent flaw in the math of the AI model.

756
00:39:22,180 --> 00:39:26,780
It was human managers failing to demand recoverability before they put the software in front of customers.

757
00:39:26,780 --> 00:39:27,380
Precisely.

758
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:31,800
It is a failure of human management, not a failure of the generation technology itself.

759
00:39:32,360 --> 00:39:34,540
The technology did exactly what it was told to do.

760
00:39:34,900 --> 00:39:36,720
The humans failed to constrain it.

761
00:39:37,080 --> 00:39:37,320
Wow.

762
00:39:37,740 --> 00:39:40,180
Okay, as we enter the final phase of this deep dive,

763
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:44,760
I want to highlight a psychological trap that Phloxian identifies from the text.

764
00:39:45,040 --> 00:39:45,520
Which one?

765
00:39:45,860 --> 00:39:50,660
I think this explains so much of the anger and exhaustion I hear from my own colleagues right now.

766
00:39:51,320 --> 00:39:55,360
Phloxian calls it visible failure and invisible production.

767
00:39:55,360 --> 00:40:00,440
It perfectly articulates the emotional whiplash of using AI in the workplace.

768
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:01,700
It really does.

769
00:40:02,080 --> 00:40:04,980
Think about how we actually experience these tools day to day.

770
00:40:05,740 --> 00:40:08,880
When an AI tool works perfectly, it is entirely invisible.

771
00:40:09,220 --> 00:40:10,140
You don't even notice it.

772
00:40:10,540 --> 00:40:13,620
It writes 50 lines of tedious boilerplate code for you.

773
00:40:13,920 --> 00:40:18,480
You hit tab, you accept it, and you move on to the interesting part of your job.

774
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:20,920
It fades completely into the background.

775
00:40:20,920 --> 00:40:26,180
Over a year, it might save you thousands of hours of unseen, monotonous labor.

776
00:40:26,180 --> 00:40:32,540
The counterfactual labor, the typing you didn't have to do, is completely unmeasured by your own emotions.

777
00:40:33,020 --> 00:40:35,320
You don't feel the absence of boredom.

778
00:40:35,600 --> 00:40:35,800
Right.

779
00:40:36,340 --> 00:40:42,860
But when the AI fails, when it confidently hallucinates a software library that doesn't exist,

780
00:40:43,340 --> 00:40:47,120
and you spend three grueling hours debugging a ghost...

781
00:40:47,120 --> 00:40:47,840
That is painful.

782
00:40:48,120 --> 00:40:50,340
...that failure screams for your attention.

783
00:40:50,340 --> 00:40:53,920
It is highly visible, highly frustrating, and deeply visceral.

784
00:40:54,660 --> 00:40:58,860
Therefore, human frustration is an awful metric for aggregate productivity.

785
00:40:59,120 --> 00:40:59,880
That is so true.

786
00:41:00,420 --> 00:41:06,100
We feel the three hours lost intensely, but our brains totally ignore the 300 hours saved.

787
00:41:06,440 --> 00:41:14,680
And what's fascinating is that this psychological distortion is perfectly mirrored by the empirical chaos in the productivity data.

788
00:41:15,120 --> 00:41:15,540
How so?

789
00:41:15,540 --> 00:41:23,760
If we look at the actual academic studies, trying to measure whether AI makes developers faster, the results are wildly contradictory.

790
00:41:24,560 --> 00:41:26,860
I know Flyxion reviews a few of these studies in the essay.

791
00:41:27,320 --> 00:41:28,860
Walk me through the timeline of the data.

792
00:41:29,640 --> 00:41:29,980
Okay.

793
00:41:30,200 --> 00:41:31,260
Let's look at the progression.

794
00:41:31,980 --> 00:41:36,900
In 2023, there was a major, highly publicized study of GitHub Copilot.

795
00:41:36,900 --> 00:41:46,480
It showed that developers using the AI assistant completed a defined, isolated task 55.8% faster than the control group.

796
00:41:46,920 --> 00:41:48,600
55.8% faster.

797
00:41:48,680 --> 00:41:50,060
I remember when that study came out.

798
00:41:50,320 --> 00:41:52,080
CEOs were practically salivating.

799
00:41:52,400 --> 00:41:55,880
That is a massive revolutionary leap in productivity.

800
00:41:56,360 --> 00:41:56,840
It is.

801
00:41:57,280 --> 00:41:59,800
But then, look at the data from 2025.

802
00:42:00,580 --> 00:42:05,040
A randomized trial by a group called METR looked at experienced developers.

803
00:42:05,380 --> 00:42:05,660
Okay.

804
00:42:05,660 --> 00:42:13,500
Instead of giving them isolated toy problems, they had them work in complex, real-world, open-source repositories that they already knew well.

805
00:42:13,800 --> 00:42:14,560
And what did they find?

806
00:42:15,080 --> 00:42:21,140
That study found that access to AI tools actually increased their completion time by roughly 19%.

807
00:42:21,140 --> 00:42:22,160
Wait, wait, wait.

808
00:42:22,360 --> 00:42:26,520
Giving experts an AI tool made them 19% slower?

809
00:42:26,920 --> 00:42:27,820
That defies logic.

810
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:28,560
How does that happen?

811
00:42:28,700 --> 00:42:33,120
Because of what we discussed earlier, the extensive margin and endogenous discovery.

812
00:42:33,380 --> 00:42:34,180
Ah, okay.

813
00:42:34,180 --> 00:42:39,900
A 2026 update from that same research group admitted that their testing methodology essentially broke down.

814
00:42:40,460 --> 00:42:40,720
Why?

815
00:42:41,160 --> 00:42:46,300
Because the human developers changed the mix of tasks they attempted once they had the AI.

816
00:42:46,700 --> 00:42:47,480
Let me guess.

817
00:42:48,100 --> 00:42:52,260
They stopped doing the easy stuff and started trying the impossibly hard stuff?

818
00:42:52,600 --> 00:42:53,080
Exactly.

819
00:42:53,840 --> 00:42:56,380
Paint the picture of a developer in that 2025 study.

820
00:42:56,380 --> 00:42:58,600
You give an expert an AI.

821
00:42:59,120 --> 00:43:03,300
They don't just sit there writing the same simple login functions 55% faster.

822
00:43:04,020 --> 00:43:04,820
No, they get ambitious.

823
00:43:05,500 --> 00:43:08,440
They look at the AI, they look at their code base, and they say, you know what?

824
00:43:08,860 --> 00:43:10,860
I've always hated our database architecture.

825
00:43:11,300 --> 00:43:14,980
With this AI, I bet I could refactor the entire backend in a week.

826
00:43:14,980 --> 00:43:19,520
They attempt massive structural changes that they never would have risked before.

827
00:43:19,840 --> 00:43:20,100
Yes.

828
00:43:20,700 --> 00:43:25,380
They are attempting vastly harder work, which naturally takes longer and involves more debugging,

829
00:43:25,640 --> 00:43:29,320
but potentially yields exponentially higher value for the software.

830
00:43:29,700 --> 00:43:33,820
So they got slower because their ambitions scaled up to meet the power of the tool.

831
00:43:33,820 --> 00:43:34,380
Yes.

832
00:43:34,820 --> 00:43:38,540
And if we zoom out to the macroeconomic level, this isn't the first time history has seen

833
00:43:38,540 --> 00:43:42,360
this kind of statistical chaos during a technological transition.

834
00:43:43,240 --> 00:43:47,580
Flyxion links this to the economics of what are called general purpose technologies.

835
00:43:47,840 --> 00:43:48,120
Okay.

836
00:43:48,680 --> 00:43:53,600
We can look at the work of economic historians like Paul David or Bresnahan and Trechtenberg.

837
00:43:54,100 --> 00:43:55,300
Give me a historical parallel.

838
00:43:56,160 --> 00:44:02,500
In 1990, Paul David published a famous paper looking at the transition from steam power to the electric

839
00:44:02,500 --> 00:44:03,000
dynamo.

840
00:44:03,000 --> 00:44:04,160
The electric dynamo.

841
00:44:04,240 --> 00:44:04,320
Yeah.

842
00:44:05,200 --> 00:44:09,520
In the late 19th century, factories were powered by giant central steam engines.

843
00:44:10,180 --> 00:44:15,300
Every machine in the factory had to be physically connected to one massive, dangerous rotating

844
00:44:15,300 --> 00:44:15,940
driveshaft.

845
00:44:16,440 --> 00:44:16,800
Okay.

846
00:44:17,040 --> 00:44:17,900
Picture that in your head.

847
00:44:18,200 --> 00:44:22,620
When electricity was first introduced, factory owners simply ripped out the steam engine and

848
00:44:22,620 --> 00:44:24,500
put one giant electric motor in its place.

849
00:44:24,500 --> 00:44:27,660
They just swapped the power source but kept the old factory layout.

850
00:44:28,240 --> 00:44:28,580
Exactly.

851
00:44:29,200 --> 00:44:31,100
And productivity statistics didn't improve.

852
00:44:31,100 --> 00:44:32,960
In many cases, they plummeted.

853
00:44:33,460 --> 00:44:36,940
It took decades for engineers to realize the true power of electricity.

854
00:44:37,640 --> 00:44:38,540
The unit drive.

855
00:44:39,200 --> 00:44:40,180
What's the unit drive?

856
00:44:40,760 --> 00:44:44,440
You could put a small dedicated electric motor on every individual machine.

857
00:44:44,880 --> 00:44:46,980
You didn't need the central driveshaft anymore.

858
00:44:46,980 --> 00:44:50,940
You could completely redesign the entire assembly line for optimal flow.

859
00:44:51,480 --> 00:44:51,940
Oh, wow.

860
00:44:52,640 --> 00:44:56,940
But while you are in the middle of tearing down the old factory and redesigning the assembly

861
00:44:56,940 --> 00:44:59,000
line, your productivity is chaotic.

862
00:44:59,360 --> 00:44:59,800
It drops.

863
00:45:00,220 --> 00:45:03,820
We are in the factory redesign phase of the AI revolution.

864
00:45:03,820 --> 00:45:09,400
We are still trying to hook AI up to the old steam-powered driveshafts of our legacy workflows.

865
00:45:10,160 --> 00:45:10,500
We are.

866
00:45:11,020 --> 00:45:15,200
And surviving this redesign phase requires an entirely new set of skills.

867
00:45:15,700 --> 00:45:20,560
Which brings us to Flyxion's ultimate conclusion about the future of human work and what he

868
00:45:20,560 --> 00:45:22,100
calls the new competence.

869
00:45:22,500 --> 00:45:23,320
The new competence.

870
00:45:23,320 --> 00:45:28,020
Because programming, as we've established, is no longer just about typing syntax.

871
00:45:28,700 --> 00:45:33,920
If the machine can produce the text instantly, what is the human engineer actually being paid to do?

872
00:45:34,320 --> 00:45:37,100
The premium human skill has fundamentally shifted.

873
00:45:37,720 --> 00:45:39,420
It is no longer text generation.

874
00:45:40,020 --> 00:45:45,580
It is now the ability to meticulously specify a problem, to evaluate the generated output,

875
00:45:45,940 --> 00:45:50,060
to rigorously validate that output against messy, real-world requirements,

876
00:45:50,060 --> 00:45:53,120
and to govern the system over its entire life cycle.

877
00:45:53,320 --> 00:45:59,800
Because AI makes experimenting so incredibly cheap, the recognized domain of ideas is going

878
00:45:59,800 --> 00:46:00,340
to explode.

879
00:46:00,920 --> 00:46:03,760
The world is about to be flooded with bad ideas.

880
00:46:04,220 --> 00:46:08,200
It is going to be raining terrible, half-baked, unmaintainable prototypes.

881
00:46:08,960 --> 00:46:13,820
The horizon of feasible work has expanded, bringing in incredible opportunities, but also

882
00:46:13,820 --> 00:46:15,920
massive catastrophic distractions.

883
00:46:16,960 --> 00:46:20,700
Therefore, human competence is now overwhelmingly about selection.

884
00:46:21,020 --> 00:46:21,500
Selection.

885
00:46:21,500 --> 00:46:26,800
Knowing what is actually worth building, and what is safe to deploy, in a world where you

886
00:46:26,800 --> 00:46:28,420
can build almost anything for pennies.

887
00:46:28,520 --> 00:46:30,860
Let's recap this incredible journey we've been on.

888
00:46:31,140 --> 00:46:36,820
We started with a viral Reddit story about an unkillable rogue chatbot, and we realized that

889
00:46:36,820 --> 00:46:39,340
you cannot judge complexity without a denominator.

890
00:46:39,340 --> 00:46:50,220
We explored the intensive versus extensive margins, realizing that cheap tools don't just make us faster, they expand the horizon of the unattempted.

891
00:46:50,360 --> 00:46:57,400
We saw how the loss of physical constraints leads to massive bloat if we don't artificially budget our complexity through mental accounting.

892
00:46:57,400 --> 00:47:02,460
We exposed the epistemic terror of a verification loop that grades its own homework.

893
00:47:02,700 --> 00:47:10,100
And ultimately, we redefined what competence looks like in an age where the typing is free, but the maintenance is forever.

894
00:47:10,100 --> 00:47:13,060
It is a profound shift in how we relate to our tools.

895
00:47:13,640 --> 00:47:17,000
And the core takeaway for you listening to this right now is highly practical.

896
00:47:17,320 --> 00:47:17,800
What is it?

897
00:47:18,360 --> 00:47:26,160
Whenever you integrate a powerful new automated system into your workflow, regardless of your industry, you must constantly pause and ask yourself two questions.

898
00:47:26,680 --> 00:47:27,760
Okay, two questions.

899
00:47:27,760 --> 00:47:35,020
First, am I maintaining the mental theory of my work, or am I just blindly collecting generated artifacts?

900
00:47:36,160 --> 00:47:41,360
And second, is my control over this process still recoverable if the automation completely fails?

901
00:47:41,800 --> 00:47:45,120
If the answer to either of those is no, you aren't building a solution.

902
00:47:45,380 --> 00:47:47,040
You are building a trap for yourself.

903
00:47:47,280 --> 00:47:47,760
Exactly.

904
00:47:48,220 --> 00:47:51,100
I want to leave you with one final provocative thought.

905
00:47:51,100 --> 00:48:00,920
Building on Flyxion's point that cheaper implementation leads to more distractions and a flood of garbage projects, think about the ultimate endgame of this technology.

906
00:48:01,300 --> 00:48:01,420
Yeah.

907
00:48:01,900 --> 00:48:08,980
If the cost of building solutions drops all the way to zero, then the solutions themselves are no longer the valuable part of the economic equation.

908
00:48:08,980 --> 00:48:17,820
In a world where an AI can build almost anything instantly, does the ultimate currency shift entirely to the people who can define a truly meaningful problem?

909
00:48:18,100 --> 00:48:19,760
That is a fascinating question.

910
00:48:19,760 --> 00:48:22,500
Think about that the next time you sit down to write a prompt.

911
00:48:23,160 --> 00:48:28,200
Are you just asking the machine for a cheap solution, or are you actually interrogating a worthy problem?

912
00:48:28,480 --> 00:48:28,940
I love that.

913
00:48:29,640 --> 00:48:31,700
And with that, we're going to circle back to where we started.

914
00:48:32,220 --> 00:48:34,400
That mechanic staring at the car that fights back.

915
00:48:35,040 --> 00:48:38,760
AI might make the diagnostic landscape of our daily work incredibly muddy.

916
00:48:39,380 --> 00:48:45,320
It might hide the jagged flaws of our broken code beneath layers of self-repairing containers and hallucinated documentation.

917
00:48:45,640 --> 00:48:48,360
But if we fight to maintain our mental theory...

918
00:48:48,360 --> 00:48:52,960
And if we rigidly insist on recoverability, we don't have to be afraid of the dark warehouse.

919
00:48:53,620 --> 00:48:55,900
We just have to be smarter about where we point the floodlights.

920
00:48:56,300 --> 00:48:56,700
Absolutely.

921
00:48:57,420 --> 00:48:59,560
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

922
00:48:59,940 --> 00:49:01,560
Keep questioning the systems around you.

923
00:49:01,800 --> 00:49:02,380
Stay curious.

924
00:49:02,640 --> 00:49:03,640
And we will see you next time.

